  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "Find the degree measure of an angle whose complement is \\(25\\%\\) of its supplement.",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(48\\)",
    "B": "\\(60\\)",
    "C": "\\(75\\)",
    "D": "\\(120\\)",
    "E": "\\(150\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The supplement of an angle is always \\(180^\\circ-90^\\circ=90^\\circ\\) greater than its complement. Also, \\(25\\%=\\frac14\\), so the supplement is four times the complement.\n\nThink of the complement as one part and the supplement as four equal parts. Their difference is three parts, so each part measures\n\\[\n90^\\circ\\div3=30^\\circ.\n\\]\nThe complement is therefore \\(30^\\circ\\), and the requested angle measures\n\\[\n90^\\circ-30^\\circ=60^\\circ.\n\\]\nIts supplement is \\(120^\\circ\\), and \\(30\\) is one quarter of \\(120\\). Thus the answer is \\(\\boxed{60^\\circ}\\), choice B.",
